Spec: ServiceScheduleBackupScript

Platform: Rhai script library (vynil-core 0.7.7 engine)

Purpose:
  Keep the periodic restic backup schedule of one `ServiceInstance` applied: invoked from the
    install lane's backup branch, render the packaged `cronbackup.yaml` partial into the
    instance's schedule objects (scripts ConfigMap, ServiceAccount, RoleBinding and a CronJob
    whose Pod dispatches the package type's own `backup` lane) and apply them, so backups ride
    the namespace maintenance-window cron. All env/volume/schedule inputs come from
    @bctx::run(instance, context, `false`) — this file computes no cron expression, reads no
    retention value and runs no restic itself. It is the CR-side stage owning
    @set_status_schedule_backup_failed and re-throws into the install lane's exit-1. Neat twin
    ./../tenant/schedule_backup.rhai, identical except the per-document apply call
    (@install_allowed_obj there).

Exposes:
  @run(@instance, @context) — the module's sole @fn and entry point (`schedule_backup.rhai` +
    `run` match the entry-point patterns of /agent/src/linting/rhai_checker.sdd); reached ONLY
    through @import_run("schedule_backup", instance, context) in ./install.rhai's backup branch
    — no Rust lane names the module, and the caller discards the return value whole
    (./install.sdd). A package `scripts/schedule_backup.rhai` shadowing the name replaces this
    whole lane on that install.

Accepts:
  @instance — the injected @ServiceInstance @Dynamic. Direct reads in this file:
    `.metadata.namespace` (the apply-namespace argument) and the @set_status_schedule_backup_failed
    method on failure. Everything else flows through collaborators on the same object:
    @bctx::run requires `.status.vitals` (array, unguarded) and `.status.scalables` (guarded);
    `spec.initFrom` is never read at this call site (third arg `false`).
  @context — the install-lane map (the same object ./install.rhai threads to every bucket).
    Direct reads: `template_dir` (unguarded property-on-`()` if non-map — ./context.rhai always
    copies it from `args`, default `./agent/templates`) and `package_dir` (the
    `${context.package_dir}/pods` gate). The whole map is also @bctx::run's data source and the
    Handlebars render data, so the shipped templates read `instance.appslug`/`.namespace`/
    `["package"].type`, `instance.selector`, `cluster.vynil_namespace`, `agent_image`, `schedule`,
    `service_account`, `envs`/`envs_from`/`volumes`/`mounts`/`files`/`has_files`/`backup_affinity`
    — that key contract is owned by ./context.sdd + ./../lib/build_context.sdd and
    ./../lib/backup_context.sdd, not here. The map's `instance` key (built view) is NOT the
    injected CR.
  Hooks — three optional @import_run module names: `schedule_backup_pre`,
    `schedule_backup_prepare`, `schedule_backup_post`, each called with the same
    (instance, context) pair. None ships in any engine directory (./, ./../tenant, ./../lib) —
    package `scripts/` slots only.

Returns:
  The `context` map as held after the try/catch, plus a `schedule_backup_post` merge on a map
    return — a value ./install.rhai discards whole (./install.sdd); the lane communicates only
    by side effects. The observable outcomes: the rendered documents applied through
    @install_obj in decoded-document order — shipped partials yield `{appslug}-backups-scripts`
    ConfigMap (only when `has_files`), ServiceAccount `{appslug}-backup`, RoleBinding
    `{appslug}-backup` (roleRef ClusterRole `${cluster.vynil_namespace}:backups`, subject SA in
    `instance.namespace`) and CronJob `{appslug}-backups` with `spec.schedule` = the `schedule`
    context text — no document carries its own `metadata.namespace`, so all four land in
    `instance.metadata.namespace`; and the `ScheduleBackup` failure condition on error. The
    CronJob's per-job labels are `instance.selector` plus `app.kubernetes.io/component=backup`
    emitted by @selector_from_ctx (/common/src/handlebarshandler.sdd) — the component label is
    what ./delete_backup.rhai's Job sweep later filters on.

Raises:
  Zero error-fabricating @throw statement: the file's one throw is the bare `throw;` re-throw
    after the failure marker. @import_run swallows only the two skip markers (`No ${name}
    module, skipping.` and `No ${name}::run function, skipping.`) — everything else propagates.
  Inside the @try (marker path): a raw raise from @bctx::run (dead/absent `status.vitals`,
    missing `instance` context key, the Cluster-branch Secrets @list_meta read — its spec's
    Raises), from either @register_partial_dir call on a broken/unreadable partial directory,
    from @render_from or @yaml_decode_multi, the engine error of a non-map document reaching
    @install_obj's `.kind` dot (no @get_objects-style qualification filter runs on this stream),
    @install_obj's `kind: ${obj.kind} is unknown from the apiServer.` throw, or an apply
    KubeError — becomes @set_status_schedule_backup_failed(reason) (verbatim string; non-string
    through @json_encode) then `throw;`: the install lane reaches no later bucket and exits 1
    (./install.sdd), condition `ScheduleBackup`/`Warning` `ScheduleBackupFailed` with truncated
    note (/common/src/instance_macros.sdd).
  A failure on the N-th document leaves documents 1..N-1 applied and the rest never applied —
    no rollback, the marker alone records it.
  Outside the @try: @set_status_schedule_backup_failed's own KubeError replaces the original
    error; a raise inside the `schedule_backup_pre` hook kills the lane with no marker at all;
    a raise inside the `schedule_backup_post` hook (the only statement after the try) likewise
    leaves no marker even though the full schedule already applied (Tasks).
  @json_encode of an unserializable caught payload in the `_` switch arm can itself raise out
    of the catch, replacing the reason (same open point as ./../lib/backup_context.sdd).

Handles:
  Stock image: all three hook names take the debug-log @import_run skip, the @type_of guard
    sees no map, and the lane runs on the unchanged `context`. A hook returning a non-map
    leaves the previous `context` in force (same merge discipline as ./install.sdd's buckets).
  Partial precedence: `template_dir` registers first, then `${package_dir}/pods` (only under
    @is_dir) registers second, so a package `pods/cronbackup.yaml.hbs` or `pods/backup.yaml.hbs`
    silently overwrites the shipped same-name partial (partial name = filename minus `.hbs`).
  The optional scripts ConfigMap is gated INSIDE the template (`{{#if has_files}}`), not by this
    file: the loop blindly applies whatever documents the stream holds.
  No dedup probe, no wait, no inventory — unlike ./init_from.sdd (Job-exist gate + @wait::all),
    every document is unconditionally re-applied on every qualifying install and every
    @install_obj return is dropped; nothing is recorded into `status`.
  Failure text shape: the marker reason is the caught engine error's own text — verbatim for
    thrown strings, @json_encode text otherwise — no wrapper or file/line prefix at this level.

Must:
  Execute exactly: @import_run("schedule_backup_pre") + map-merge guard → @try { @bctx::run
    (instance, context, `false`) → @import_run("schedule_backup_prepare") + map-merge →
    @new_hbs → @register_partial_dir(`context.template_dir`) → @is_dir-gated
    @register_partial_dir(`${context.package_dir}/pods`) → @yaml_decode_multi of @render_from
    (`"{{> cronbackup.yaml }}"`, @context) → @dir::install_obj(obj, `true`,
    instance.metadata.namespace, `false`) per document in stream order } → on raise
    @set_status_schedule_backup_failed + `throw;` → @import_run("schedule_backup_post") +
    map-merge → return `context`; nothing else, in no other order.
  Call @bctx::run with @use_init_from `false` — the schedule-lane posture per
    ./../lib/backup_context.sdd: `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` never emitted, `envs_from` stays the
    `backup-settings` Secret, per-Secret `/secrets/` volumes mounted, and `schedule` set to the
    cron text `${namespace.maintenance_start_minut} ${namespace.maintenance_start_hour} * * *`
    — this is the whole cron schedule computation: NO arithmetic, no `RESTIC_MAX_*` retention and
    no cron-expression building happens in this file or by this call site (retention defaults to
    `7`/`5`/`12`/`4` only later, inside the dispatched Pod's ./backup.sdd @from_args half — see
    the produce/consume drift recorded in ./../lib/backup_context.sdd Tasks).
  Keep the apply posture of ./../lib/install_from_dir.sdd: single-object @install_obj per
    document, `allow_cluster` `true` (inert with the shipped documents — no applied kind is
    cluster-scoped; the ClusterRole is only roleRef-referenced), `force_ns` `false` (documents
    carry no `metadata.namespace`, so one namespace applies: `instance.metadata.namespace`),
    and NO first/middle/last bucket-ordering pass — decoded stream order is apply order.
  Treat idempotence as bare full replay: there is no prior-schedule read, diff or prune; each
    qualifying install re-renders from the fresh `context` (so maintenance-window changes flow
    to the next install's CronJob) and re-applies the same fixed names. Removal lives only in
    ./delete_backup.rhai when ./install.sdd's backup gate flips.
  Write the failure marker with the two switched arms exactly as written (thrown string verbatim
    / other error `json_encode`d) and re-throw; success is never marked — no
    `set_status_*` success call, condition or tag exists for this lane; the install close in
    ./install.sdd stamps `Ready`/`tag` later.
  Emit no @log_info, @log_debug, @log_warn or @log_error line at this level (the file contains
    zero log calls; only imported collaborators log).
  Keep the import-time surface at the two top-level imports — `"backup_context"` as `bctx`,
    `"install_from_dir"` as `dir`, both import-pure per their specs — so module load touches no
    env, filesystem or cluster; a resolver miss on either is an eval error in the install lane
    before @run starts.
  Resolve the module and its three hook names against the four resolver directories frozen by
    the install Rust lane ({package_dir}/scripts first), so packages can ship the hooks or
    shadow the whole lane.

Must not:
  Open or close a maintenance/scale window: no `maintenance_start`/`maintenance_stop`
    @import_run here (unlike ./backup.sdd and ./restore.sdd) — installing a schedule perturbs
    no workload; only the cron-fired Pod quiesces.
  Read `instance.spec.initFrom` or default any restic env: the `false` third arg is the only
    lever this lane takes on the env contract that ./../lib/backup_context.sdd owns; a second
    reader (or the init lane's `true` posture leaking here) would fork source selection.
  Persist a schedule inventory into `status` (no applied-object list, no success condition):
    the counterpart stage lanes store their buckets via the @impl_instance_befores! setters —
    this lane deliberately does not, and removal matches by fixed name/labels instead.

Depends on:
  ./../lib/backup_context.rhai — the @run(..., `false`) half of the env/context contract
    (./../lib/backup_context.sdd, which freezes this file as one of its two schedule-lane
    callers).
  ./../lib/install_from_dir.rhai — @install_obj single-object apply and its raise surface
    (./../lib/install_from_dir.sdd); no other function of the module is used.
  /agent/templates/cronbackup.yaml.hbs (+ nested /agent/templates/backup.yaml.hbs Pod template)
    resolved through `context.template_dir` — the rendered manifest shape is template-owned, not
    frozen here.
  vynil-core 0.7.7 engine API — the @import_run shim, @new_hbs + @register_partial_dir +
    @render_from of the `"HandleBars"` type (/common/src/handlebarshandler.sdd, helpers
    `json_to_str`/`selector_from_ctx`/`to_json` riding the same engine), @is_dir,
    @yaml_decode_multi, @type_of, @json_encode; the `switch type_of(e)` arms are plain rhai.
  /common/src/instanceservice.sdd + /common/src/instance_macros.sdd — the
    @set_status_schedule_backup_failed bridge and the `ScheduleBackup`/`ScheduleBackupFailed`
    condition it patches; /common/src/rhaihandler.sdd — the @Script constructor mounting all of
    the above.

References:
  ./install.rhai + ./install.sdd — the sole caller: the backup branch gate (status vitals +
    `use_backup` + readable `backup-settings` Secret), the (instance, context) injection and the
    returned-context discard frozen there; /agent/src/service/install.sdd — the exit-1 error
    lane every raise here lands in, and the `args`/resolver-directory setup behind `context`.
  ./backup.sdd — the Pod-side lane the rendered cron dispatches (`agent <package.type> backup`):
    it consumes, via @from_args, the envs `bctx::run` baked into the Pod spec here.
  ./delete_backup.rhai — the cleanup lane matching these objects by name/labels (spec pending);
    see Tasks for a name-mismatch finding at that seam.
  ./init_from.sdd — sibling CR-side stage with the opposite posture (dedup + wait + its own
    marker); contrast only.
  ./../tenant/schedule_backup.rhai — diff-verified twin bar the apply call (unspecified).
  /agent/tests/rhai_lifecycle.rs — nearest lane test file; no test names this module (Tasks).

Scenario: qualifying install re-applies the whole schedule
  Given the install backup branch fired, a context with `instance.appslug` `shop`, one
    scripts file under `${package_dir}/scripts` (`has_files` true), maintenance fields `30`/`2`
    and no `pods/` dir
  When @run executes
  Then the four documents apply through @install_obj into `instance.metadata.namespace` in
    stream order: ConfigMap `shop-backups-scripts`, ServiceAccount `shop-backup`, RoleBinding
    `shop-backup`, CronJob `shop-backups` with `spec.schedule` `30 2 * * *`
  And @run returns `context`, which the caller discards, and no `status` write happens
  No mapped test.

Scenario: any raise becomes the marker and aborts the install
  Given @bctx::run dies on absent `status.vitals`, OR a document's kind is unknown to discovery,
    OR the CronJob apply hits a Forbidden
  When the raise enters the @catch
  Then the reason string is the error's own text (or its @json_encode) and
    @set_status_schedule_backup_failed patches `ScheduleBackup`/`Warning` `ScheduleBackupFailed`
  And the bare `throw;` crosses @import_run (not a skip marker), so no `install_post`, no
    `Ready`-close and exit 1; documents applied before a mid-loop failure stay applied
  No mapped test.

Scenario: package pods partial shadows the shipped one
  Given `${package_dir}/pods/cronbackup.yaml.hbs` ships a rewritten schedule section
  When @run registers partial directories
  Then the second registration wins, the package text renders instead of
    /agent/templates/cronbackup.yaml.hbs, and the loop applies whatever it yields — same
    posture for `pods/backup.yaml.hbs` replacing the Pod template
  No mapped test.

Scenario: hooks merge or skip without touching the schedule
  Given no hook module exists anywhere on the resolver path
  When @run executes
  Then all three names debug-log their skip, no @type_of guard ever merges, and the lane runs
    on the untouched `context`
  But given a package `schedule_backup_prepare.rhai` whose run returns a map, a `pods`-style
    context tweak reaches the render
  And given that hook instead raises, the marker path writes `ScheduleBackupFailed` and re-throws
  No mapped test.

Example:
  Marker posture: thrown string `boom` → reason `boom`; thrown map → reason `{"k":"v"}` json text
  Schedule text: `namespace.maintenance_start_minut: "30"` + `..._hour: "2"` → CronJob
    `schedule: 30 2 * * *` (composed in @bctx::run, not here)

Tasks:
  [ ] Map the four Scenarios to tests in /agent/tests/rhai_lifecycle.rs (or a dedicated
      rhai_backup_schedule.rs): no test names this module today; a mock engine needs a vitals-
      bearing instance, template-dir fixtures and mock applies to pin order, marker and shadow.
  [?] RoleBinding/ServiceAccount name seam: this lane creates roleRef-bound RoleBinding
      `{appslug}-backup` (singular) and ServiceAccount `{appslug}-backup`, while
      ./delete_backup.rhai deletes a RoleBinding named `{appslug}-backups` (plural, always
      NotFound-swallowed) and never sweeps the ServiceAccount — disabling backup leaves both
      behind. Decide whether the fix lands in the template (plural names) or in delete_backup.
  [?] Hook raises outside the @try (`schedule_backup_pre`, `schedule_backup_post`) abort with no
      marker — pre means "nothing scheduled, nothing recorded", post means "schedule applied,
      unrecorded, install failed". Decide whether the hooks join the guarded body (init_from.sdd
      carries the same open point for its `init_from_post`).
  [?] The hook-merged context (and the final `context`) reaches no consumer: ./install.sdd discards
      this lane's return whole and installs neither `install_post` merge nor a status write feeds
      on it. Confirm the hooks are intended as side-effect-only slots (mirrors the open
      return-discarded decision in ./install.sdd Tasks).
  [?] `ScheduleBackup` has no success twin in /common/src/instance_macros.sdd: a correctly
      scheduled instance never carries a positive condition, and reruns cannot distinguish
      "applied by a prior install" from an operator-edit of the same-named objects. Confirm the
      no-inventory posture is intended or add a success stamp.

Done when:
  Every Scenario carries a passing mock-engine test or a recorded decision, the render→apply
    order, the `false` @bctx::run posture and the single marker call still match the source, and
    `specdd lint` is green on this file.
